/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A traverse is a survey made of connected straight lines.
   - A closed traverse starts and ends at the same station.
   - In all three types, the field record is a traverse table.
   - A compass gives the bearing of every line directly.
   - Take a line of length l metres.
   - l is the line length in metres; θ is the whole circle bearing in degrees, clockwise from north; latitude is positive north and negative south; departure is positive east and negative west.
   - The sign follows the quadrant.
   - A reduced bearing α is the acute angle from the nearer north or south end of the meridian, measured towards east or west.
   - A traverse table has one row per line.
   - Coordinates come from those sums.
*/
