/* book exam traps: Using a reduced bearing in l cos θ and l sin θ. The formula needs the whole circle bearing θ; a reduced bearing gives the wrong sign in the south or west half. | Forgetting that latitude is north-south and departure is east-west. Swapping them turns every coordinate step by 90°. | Dropping the sign when adding. Latitudes and departures are signed vector components, not plain lengths, so −127.28 m must stay negative in the sum. | Applying the zero-sum check to an open traverse. Only a closed traverse must return to the start; an open traverse has no such condition. | Treating a nonzero sum as proof that the fieldwork is wrong. It is a misclosure, adjusted as shown in closing error and traverse adjustment; the sum itself must still be computed and reported. */
